    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0420" n="406" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg406"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EAC/11–1444</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stettinius</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-11-14">November 14, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row>
                        <cell rendition="#top #nowrap">Present:</cell>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Major General Hilldring, Civil Affairs
                            Division, War Department; Colonel Cutter, War Department; Mr.
                                Matthews—EUR;<anchor n="75" xml:id="d207-f1"/> Mr.
                                Hamilton—SA/HA;<anchor n="76" xml:id="d207-f2"/> Mr.
                                Leverich—CE;<anchor n="77" xml:id="d207-f3"/> Mr. Lynch—U<anchor
                                n="78" xml:id="d207-f4"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Mr. McCloy called on the Under Secretary this morning to discuss a letter to Mr.
                    Stettinius from Lord Halifax dated November 10. The letter suggested that our
                    interim directive for the treatment of Germany (JCS 1067)<anchor n="78a"
                        xml:id="d207-f5"/> be circulated to EAC.</p>
                <p>It was agreed to do this after the paper had been changed to make it tripartite
                    in nature rather than a directive addressed only to SCAEF.</p>
                <pb facs="0421" n="407" xml:id="pg407"/>
                <p>Mr. McCloy said that he would make the necessary alterations in the paper and
                    would send us a copy for ultimate transmission to Ambassador Winant.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
